WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.670
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: If you are
ingesting large amount of data

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:04.890
for analysis or
looking to simplify

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.530
the development of event
driven applications,

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.380
then you need to check
out Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:12.684
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:19.470
In the last couple episodes,
we learned about Cloud Pub/Sub

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:22.800
through Cat-Out's asynchronous
order processing website.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.310
We saw how Pub/Sub helps manage
the interactions between order,

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:29.250
packaging, shipping, and
notification services.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:32.310
In today's episode, let's
build a Cloud Pub/Sub system

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:33.270
for Cat-Out.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.600
As seen in the last episode
for Cat-Out's application,

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.930
the whole system will comprise
of three topics corresponding

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.450
to order, packaging,
and shipping service

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:44.380
for the sake of understanding.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:47.160
Let's only consider the order
service sending messages

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.350
to packaging and
notification service

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.670
and build that
part of the system.

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:54.525
Feel free to extend this further
and build the whole flow.

00:00:54.525 --> 00:00:57.870
All right, so we will have
a publisher application,

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:02.160
Order Service, which sends a
simple message, Order Received.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.730
This message gets sent to two
subscribers-- the packaging

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:08.280
service and the
notification service, which

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:09.600
process the message.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.330
And then these services
process and send the message

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.970
forward to their
topics and subscribers

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:18.790
to complete the order flow
and process to order fully.

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:21.750
OK, so now that we know
what we are building,

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:24.640
we are going to build
this in four steps.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.520
Let's start with
step one where we

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.107
will create the Cloud Pub/Sub
topic for the order service

00:01:30.107 --> 00:01:32.440
and the two subscriptions for
packaging and notification

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.470
service.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.650
If you are new to Google
Cloud, create a free account

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:40.830
by navigating to
cloud.google.com/free

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:43.290
and following the
instructions there.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:46.170
Then navigate to the
console and create or use

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:47.580
an existing project.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:50.070
Make note of the project ID.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:51.990
Now, let's navigate
to the Cloud Pub/Sub

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:56.150
subsection of the console
and click on Create a Topic.

00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:59.500
Let's call this
topic order_topic.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.070
Our order service,
which is our publisher,

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.600
will send messages
to this topic.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.960
Then scroll down
on the Create Topic

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.660
and click on Create
Subscription.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.860
Let's name the first
subscription as package_sub.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:15.870
Leave the rest as
default, which will

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.480
create a pool subscription.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.490
We use the same
procedure to create

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:23.640
another subscription attached
to the order topic called

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.620
notification sub.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:28.380
At this point, your
Cloud Pub/Sub environment

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:32.280
is ready with the order topic
and the two subscriptions.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:34.490
Now, we are onto
step two where we

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:37.590
will create a service account
that the applications use

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:41.490
for authentication and set
up Cloud IAM permissions.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:44.550
Navigate to Service Account
Section of the console

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.910
and click Create Service
Account, enter a service

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:52.110
account name, such as Pub/Sub
System, and click Create.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.090
For this example,
the service account

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:56.710
needs publishing and
subscribing permissions.

00:02:56.710 --> 00:03:00.060
So we use the select a
[? rule ?] dropdown to add

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:03.030
the Cloud Pub/Sub Publisher
and Cloud Pub/Sub Subscriber

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:05.070
[? rule. ?] Click Continue.

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:09.270
And we do not need to grant user
access to this service account.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:10.890
Then create key.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.350
This key is used by
the Client Library

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:15.870
to access the Cloud Pub/Sub API.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:18.210
Select JSON and click Create.

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:20.640
The key is sent to
the Downloads Folder.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.760
For the purpose of this demo,
we can leave it there and rename

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:26.670
the key file to key.JSON.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:28.950
Now, the next step
is to start the three

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:30.630
independent applications.

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:33.030
One, publisher, which
is our order service,

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:36.270
and two subscribers, which are
our packaging and notification

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:37.110
service.

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:40.320
For this step, we need the
Google Cloud SDK installed.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:43.800
If you don't have it, today's
the day to download it.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.080
If you're not already
in the Pub/Sub project

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:49.020
on Google Cloud SDK, then
set the project using

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.620
G Cloud Config set project.

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:54.060
List the topics to
see the order topic

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:57.390
we created from the console
and list subscriptions

00:03:57.390 --> 00:04:00.780
to see the packaging and
notifications subscriptions.

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:03.240
If you don't have Python,
this would be a great point

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:06.210
to install Python and set
up virtual environment using

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:08.460
the steps in the
description below.

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:11.640
Create a folder where you want
to download the sample Python

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.250
files for this demo.

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:15.960
cd into it and
download the code.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.000
Now, open three new terminals.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.600
One for our order publisher
and two for our subscribers.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:24.630
In these terminals, create
a Python virtual environment

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:26.250
and activate it.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:29.730
Upgrade the Google Cloud
Pub/Sub in all three terminals

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.910
and associate the JSON key with
the service account created

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:34.120
earlier.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:36.000
Now set up an
environment variable

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.370
with our current project ID.

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:41.820
We use the G Cloud command to
determine currently selected

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:45.390
project ID and set it
as the project variable.

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:49.430
Verify that the project variable
is set to our current project

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:50.550
ID.

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:52.980
cd into the directory
with sample code

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:56.460
and get into our
publisher file, pub.py.

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:00.120
Change the message from hello
world to order received.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:02.800
Now, we are ready
to send messages.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.230
Let's start our
packaging subscriber now,

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:08.910
which is Listening and
our order publisher.

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:12.540
Our order application sent
an order received message

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:15.450
with a message ID
while being unaware

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:18.150
of any existing subscriptions.

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:20.250
The packaging
subscriber application

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:24.060
receives the order received
message and prints it

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:27.240
and sends an acknowledgment
to Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:29.430
Pub/Sub receives
this acknowledgment,

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:32.670
which indicates that the message
has been processed successfully

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:36.570
and does not need to be sent
to the packaging subscriber.

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:39.090
Cloud Pub/Sub then
removes the message

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:42.010
from that subscriber backlog.

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:45.440
Now, let's start the
notification subscriber.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:48.500
And as soon as it is started,
it receives the message

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:50.510
published by the order topic.

00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:52.790
The difference here is
that this subscriber

00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:56.270
isn't started until after the
publisher has sent the message

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:58.460
to the topic and subscriptions.

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:01.700
If the notification service was
called directly from the order

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:03.620
service, then the
message would either

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:05.930
have to wait a long
time or time out

00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:08.410
since the service
was not available.

00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:10.550
Cloud Pub/Sub
manages this process

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:13.640
by effectively saving
the message for the order

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:15.720
notification subscription.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:18.290
Today, we built a simple
Cloud Pub/Sub system

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:19.880
and learned how
it sends messages

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:22.310
from a publisher
to a subscriber.

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:24.110
At this point, you
should be able to build

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:26.660
a simple application
with Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:29.750
In the upcoming videos, we will
cover more important details

00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:32.720
and choices you will need to
make when building a real world

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:34.820
application.

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:37.190
That's all for today
on Pub/Sub Made Easy.

